  <section id="why" aria-labelledby="why-h">
    <h2 id="why-h">Why Fiber Matters</h2>
    <p>Higher fiber intake is linked with lower risk of heart disease, type 2 diabetes and colorectal cancer, and with lower body weight. A large review for the World Health Organization found the biggest benefits at 25&ndash;29&nbsp;g a day or more. Fiber feeds gut bacteria, slows sugar absorption and helps you feel full, which makes it useful for fat loss too (see the <a href="/macro-calculator/">macro calculator</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="increase" aria-labelledby="increase-h">
    <h2 id="increase-h">How to Eat More Fiber</h2>
    <p>Add 3&ndash;5&nbsp;g a day each week rather than all at once, and drink more water as you do (see the <a href="/water-intake-calculator/">water intake calculator</a>). Easy swaps: whole grains for refined, beans in soups and salads, fruit with the skin on, a handful of nuts or seeds, and vegetables at two meals a day.</p>
  </section>

